---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Waitlist from '../components/Waitlist.astro';
import CompareBars from '../components/CompareBars.astro';
import VendorCard from '../components/VendorCard.astro';
import WorkerLadder from '../components/WorkerLadder.astro';
import { plans, vendors, soloVendor } from '../data/pricing';
---
<Base title="Pricing" description="Lakelet pricing: free on the laptop, $24 per person for a shared catalog, burst compute at cost plus 15% with a hard cap. Compared line by line with Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks and MotherDuck.">

  <div class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow"># pricing</div>
    <h1 class="page">Free where the work happens. Paid where it can't.</h1>
    <p class="lede">The laptop is the warehouse, and the laptop is free. You pay for two things only: a catalog your team shares, and minutes of a bigger machine when a run needs one. Every burst shows its maximum cost before it starts, and cannot exceed it.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="plans">
    <div class="grid3">
      {plans.map(p => (
        <div class="panel plan">
          <b class="price">{p.price}{p.unit && <small>{p.unit}</small>}</b>
          <h3>{p.name}</h3>
          <div class="tag">{p.tag}</div>
          <ul>{p.features.map(([strong, rest]) => <li>{strong && <b>{strong}</b>}{strong && ' '}{rest}</li>)}</ul>
          <p class="foot" set:html={p.foot} />
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

<section id="workers">
    <div class="eyebrow"># burst worker sizes</div>
    <h2 class="sec">What a burst actually costs.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Workers are AWS Fargate tasks today. Prices below are AWS list, on-demand, us-east-1, before Lakelet's 15%. Spot follows the beta and will bring them down further.</p>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="panel card">
        <h3>Size ladder</h3>
        <p>The gauge picks the smallest size whose memory is 1.5× the run's estimated peak.</p>
        <WorkerLadder detailed />
        <small>Fargate list: $0.04048 per vCPU-hour and $0.004445 per GB-hour. S3 request charges are passed through at cost. Cold start on Fargate is 30–60 s and is billed; the Team warm pool removes it.</small>
      </div>
      <div class="panel card">
        <h3>How the cap works</h3>
        <p>Before a burst, the gauge estimates run time on the chosen worker. The cap is that estimate times two, rounded up to a clean number, and you see it before you confirm.</p>
        <p>The control plane converts the cap into a wall-clock budget for that worker size. The worker's agent kills DuckDB when the budget is reached and returns a partial-progress error. You can raise the cap deliberately; it cannot be exceeded accidentally.</p>
        <p>For scheduled and agent-driven runs, the cap comes from <code>lakelet.toml</code> (per project) or <code>LAKELET_AGENT_CAP_USD</code> (per agent, per day). Past the daily cap, agent calls are refused with the remaining budget.</p>
        <small>Nothing runs in the cloud without a number on the screen and a click, or a number in a config file you wrote.</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="compare">
    <div class="eyebrow"># the same month, five ways</div>
    <h2 class="sec">One data team of five. Same tables, same runs, five bills.</h2>
    <div class="sub">
      <p>To compare honestly we modeled one workload and priced it at every vendor's public list price. The workload: a five-person team, 38 GB of Iceberg tables in S3, 1,240 dbt runs a month (about 40 a day, hourly during business hours), and a handful of dashboards that keep a warehouse awake roughly eight hours a day.</p>
      <p>These are model numbers, not quotes. Your bill depends on how long your warehouse stays awake, which is the thing Lakelet removes.</p>
    </div>

    
    <CompareBars heading={false} note="Monthly, USD, list prices as of September 2026. Assumptions and sources below." />
    <div class="vendors">
      {vendors.map(v => <VendorCard vendor={v} />)}
      <VendorCard vendor={soloVendor} />
    </div>
    <div class="honest">
      <h3>Where Lakelet is not the cheapest</h3>
      <p><b>Tiny teams on BigQuery.</b> Under a few hundred GB with modest scan volume, BigQuery's free TiB and no-warehouse billing can land near or below a Team subscription. Lakelet's case there is control and portability, not price: your files stay Iceberg in your bucket, and nothing decides for you.</p>
      <p><b>Above a terabyte or two.</b> When most runs need more than a laptop, most runs burst, and you are paying for cloud compute plus 15%. At that point a reserved warehouse can be cheaper per hour. Lakelet still avoids idle time, but the free tier stops doing most of the work.</p>
      <p><b>Heavy concurrent BI.</b> Fifty people refreshing dashboards all day is a warehouse-shaped workload. Lakelet serves it by publishing models to your bucket and letting your BI tool read Iceberg directly, which works, but it is not what the gauge was built for.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

<section id="faq">
    <div class="eyebrow"># billing questions</div>
    <h2 class="sec">Questions people ask before they put in a card.</h2>
    <div class="faq" style="margin-top:24px">
      <details><summary>Is there a minimum, a commitment, or a per-seat floor on Burst?</summary><p>No. Burst is billed per second of worker time, per run, with no minimum. You can be on the free Local tier and burst once a year.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can the bill ever exceed the cap?</summary><p>No. The cap is enforced by the worker itself, not by an alert after the fact. When the budget is reached the run is killed and you get a partial-progress error and a bill at or under the cap. Raise the cap and rerun if you want it to finish.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What happens if a spot worker is reclaimed, once spot ships?</summary><p>The run is retried once on on-demand capacity within the same cap. If it still cannot finish inside the cap, it fails and you are billed only for time used.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I run workers in my own AWS account?</summary><p>Not in the beta: workers run on Lakelet's account, in your bucket's region. Bring-your-own-account bursts, which run in your account and appear on your AWS bill with Lakelet's 15% on the compute it orchestrated, come later. That will keep data, compute and billing inside your boundary.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What does Team store on your side?</summary><p>Catalog metadata (table schemas, snapshot pointers, commit history), job specs and metrics. Never data files. Delete your Team workspace and your bucket still contains a complete, readable Iceberg lakehouse.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What changes when the beta ends?</summary><p>Early Team pricing is locked for anyone who joins during the private beta. Local stays free and open source; that is the point of the product, not a promotion.</p></details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="final dark" id="waitlist">
    <h2 class="sec">Send us your last warehouse bill. We'll show you the Lakelet version.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Developer preview on macOS and Linux, open source today. Free forever on the laptop.</p>
    <Waitlist dark />
  </section>

  <section class="sources-sec">
    <p class="sources"><b>Sources for the comparison</b> (list prices, September 2026): Snowflake credit pricing by edition and warehouse credit rates (Standard $2, Enterprise $3, Business Critical $4; X-Small = 1 credit/hr, each size doubles) and storage ~$23/TB; BigQuery on-demand $6.25/TiB with 1 TiB free monthly, active storage $0.02/GB; Databricks Serverless SQL ~$0.70/DBU (US), 2X-Small = 4 DBU/hr; MotherDuck Lite (free, 3 users, 10 GB, 10 compute-hours), Business $250/month per org plus usage, Pulse $0.60/hr, storage $0.04/GB-month (pricing changed Jan/Feb 2026); AWS Fargate on-demand $0.04048/vCPU-hr and $0.004445/GB-hr, S3 Standard $0.023/GB-month. Warehouse-awake hours, scan volumes and burst counts are modeling assumptions and are stated in each card. Not affiliated with any vendor named here.</p>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .hero{padding:40px 0 48px}
  .hero h1{max-width:18ch}
  .plan{padding:24px;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
  .plan b.price{display:block;font-size:34px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.02em;line-height:1}
  .plan b.price small{font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:0;margin-left:4px}
  .plan h3{font-size:19px;margin:12px 0 4px}
  .plan .tag{font:500 11px var(--mono);color:var(--lake);margin-bottom:10px}
  .plan ul{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:8px 0 0;display:grid;gap:8px;font-size:14px;color:var(--muted)}
  .plan li::before{content:"·";color:var(--lake);font-weight:800;margin-right:8px}
  .plan li b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
  .plan .foot{margin-top:auto;padding-top:16px;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)}
  .card{padding:22px}
  .card h3{font-size:17px;margin-bottom:8px}
  .card p{color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-bottom:12px}
  .card small{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;margin-top:10px}
  .vendors{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;margin-top:20px}
  .vendors>*{min-width:0}
  .sources-sec{border-top:0;padding-top:28px}
  .sources{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7}
  @media (max-width:820px){ .vendors{grid-template-columns:1fr} }
</style>
